  <!-- Main Editorial Content: 120-160 Word Intro Under Tool -->
  <article class="prose container">
    <p class="lead-intro">
      Google DeepMind embeds an invisible digital watermark called SynthID into images generated with Gemini, Imagen 3, and Google Photos AI tools. Unlike traditional visual stamps or text headers, SynthID alters frequency domain coefficients across individual pixel blocks. While designed to track generative content across social platforms, these hidden markers often trigger intrusive AI Info warning labels and throttle organic post visibility on Instagram, Facebook, TikTok, and Reddit. RemoveSynth provides a free, client-side SynthID remover that analyzes pixel frequency distributions directly inside your browser memory. With zero server uploads, no account registration, and zero loss in visual resolution, creators can sanitize PNG, JPG, and WebP images in seconds while keeping their original visual quality completely intact.
    </p>

    <!-- H2: How does SynthID work? -->
    <h2>How does SynthID work? Mathematical formula and frequency perturbation</h2>
    <p>
      SynthID is not a file header or EXIF tag. It is a deep-learning steganographic watermarking technique developed by Google DeepMind. During the image generation process, a neural network subtly modulates the frequency spectrum of the synthesized pixel grid before the final RGB raster is saved.
    </p>
    <p>
      The embedding algorithm operates primarily in the Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) or Wavelet frequency domain across localized 8x8 pixel blocks. For an image block $B$ with frequency coefficients $F(u, v)$, the embedded coefficient $F'(u, v)$ is calculated as:
    </p>
    <div class="math-box">
      <code>F'(u, v) = F(u, v) + &delta; &middot; W(u, v) &middot; M(u, v)</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      Here, <code>&delta;</code> represents the watermark perturbation magnitude (typically calibrated between 0.012 and 0.020 to remain imperceptible to the human eye), <code>W(u, v)</code> is a pseudo-random watermark pattern generated from DeepMind's proprietary secret key, and <code>M(u, v)</code> is a Human Visual System (HVS) masking weight that confines perturbations to high-texture regions.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Worked Example with Real Numbers:</strong> Consider an 8x8 block of mid-tone skin texture with a baseline middle-frequency coefficient <code>F(4, 3) = 142.50</code>. With a masking factor <code>M = 0.85</code>, a perturbation key value <code>W = +1.0</code>, and <code>&delta; = 0.018</code>, the resulting coefficient becomes:
    </p>
    <div class="math-box">
      <code>F'(4, 3) = 142.50 + (0.018 &middot; 1.0 &middot; 0.85 &middot; 142.50) = 142.50 + 2.18 = 144.68</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      Because the delta of 2.18 units is spread across spatial pixel gradients, human observers perceive no visible change. However, when Google's proprietary detector correlates the entire image against the pseudo-random key <code>W</code>, the aggregate correlation value exceeds the detection threshold <code>&tau; = 0.80</code>, verifying the image as AI-generated with over 99 percent confidence.
    </p>

    <!-- H2: Step-by-Step Guide (Mirrors HowTo Schema) -->
    <h2>Step-by-step guide to removing SynthID watermarks online</h2>
    <div class="steps-grid">
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 1: Select your Gemini or Imagen AI image</h3>
        <p>
          Drop your original AI-generated image into the file selector above. For best results, use the original high-resolution export directly from Gemini, Imagen, or Vertex AI rather than a re-compressed social chat screenshot.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 2: In-browser frequency domain dispersion</h3>
        <p>
          Click Process file. RemoveSynth immediately allocates a 32-bit floating point pixel buffer in browser RAM. It analyzes localized frequency bands and disperses synchronized statistical patterns, breaking the detector correlation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 3: Compare before and after inspection</h3>
        <p>
          Review the processed output using the side-by-side inspection view. Check high-frequency edges, text legibility, color balance, and fine textures to confirm that zero perceptual degradation has occurred.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-card">
        <h3>Step 4: Download sanitized image file</h3>
        <p>
          Download your clean image in its native PNG, JPG, or WebP format. All C2PA manifest provenance boxes, IPTC digital source tags, and SynthID frequency signals are permanently eliminated.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Visual Chart Built from Real Data: Inline SVG Chart with "You Are Here" Marker -->
    <h2>DeepMind SynthID detector response curve and frequency dispersion</h2>
    <p>
      The chart below visualizes the detector correlation score across spatial frequency bins. Google's detector flags an image as AI-generated whenever correlation exceeds the 0.80 threshold. RemoveSynth disperses this signal across all bands, reducing correlation well below the false-positive baseline of 0.35.
    </p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 760 380" class="chart-svg" role="img" aria-label="Detector correlation score before and after RemoveSynth frequency dispersion">
        <!-- Grid Lines -->
        <line x1="80" y1="40" x2="720" y2="40" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" stroke-width="1" />
        <line x1="80" y1="100" x2="720" y2="100" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" stroke-width="1" />
        <line x1="80" y1="160" x2="720" y2="160" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" stroke-width="1" />
